GoldLink's change to melonDS (UC11): the steady overworld draws what the camera follows on a layer
of its own, so it and the world are both exact at any speed.

The world is drawn at a fixed phase within a pixel and the window slides the picture back by its
shift at the screen's resolution. The player moves across the world by fractions of a pixel
(walking up 1.5 a scene, running 3, surfing and slow walks other fractions), and the DS draws at
whole pixels only, so with one slide per picture either the world or the player jumped half a
pixel (the user's play test, 2026-11-14: the player stuttered walking up and down once the world
didn't).

- GPU3D: at every swap MidClassify finds the objects that stay put on screen while the camera
  moves (under half its step: the player, their shadow, a bobbing surf mount, a follower walking
  straight behind; the same match as the pictures in between, by key, then place, as people's
  sprites share keys). From the next scene each is drawn with its origin at the same clip
  coordinates every frame: the largest (the reference) at a fixed phase within a pixel, the rest
  at their offsets from it in whole pixels, kept until 3/4 of a pixel off (the camera's and the
  shifts' rounding, a thousandth of a pixel, otherwise flipped single texels of the sprite from
  frame to frame: the user's play test, Leroy's hair); they're flagged (Polygon::Attached) and the layer's shift (AttachSlide)
  goes with the frame like OrthoShift. The picture in between draws them at the same targets;
  patch 0021 moves the world exactly halfway.
- GPU3D_Soft: an attached object's pixels carry attribute bit 30 (opaque) or 31 (translucent,
  kept under later translucent ones); edge marking compares the polygon IDs alone (bits 24-29).
  Its translucent pixels (its shadow) aren't blended into the picture but kept aside with their
  alpha (AttachTrans, then ShownAttachTrans with the depth; an opaque pixel over one clears it):
  blended in, the ground under the shadow moved with the player's layer instead of the world and
  flickered.

Applied by build.rs to a copy of the submodule (which stays at upstream).

